---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="The System · San Lee"
  description="How a single LLM classifier grew into a four-repo system, and the seam-level discipline I put on it: frozen contracts, idempotency, and recorded decisions."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/the-system.html"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/assets/og-the-system.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Contract-seam plate (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: both sides
       report GREEN against their own copy of the shape, and the wire
       between them is broken. Not a second system diagram (the interactive
       map below already is that). Max-width 260 keeps viewBox units 1:1
       with CSS px (same discipline as .two-fail / .judge-plate). */
    .seam-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 8px;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-edge-break {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    /* GREEN: ink solid — the report each side believed. Word + stroke. */
    .seam-plate .sp-ok {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-ok-text {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    /* BROKEN: oxide — the seam. Word + oxide, not colour-only. */
    .seam-plate .sp-bad {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-bad-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .seam-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .seam-plate marker path.sp-head-bad { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    .seam-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0 0 18px;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="the-system" />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="../index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>The System</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">How a single classifier grew into a four-repo system, and the discipline I put on the seams as it did.</p>

    <p class="lead">
      This did not start as a system. It started as one project, the defense-news
      classifier. A notes API, a RAG agent, and a knowledge base came later. Once
      there were four repos that had to talk to each other, I had a choice: let
      them fuse into one tangled codebase, or impose discipline on the seams
      between them. I chose discipline and recorded why at each step.
    </p>

    <!-- Argument: each side's test is green against its own copy of the
         shape; the wire between them is broken. Word + geometry first
         (GREEN / GREEN / BROKEN), oxide only on the seam. ADR-010 N/A. -->
    <svg class="seam-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 148" role="img"
         aria-label="Contract seam: the provider tests its own fixture and reports GREEN. The consumer tests its own stub and reports GREEN. The wire between them is BROKEN, because each side asserted against its own copy of the shape.">
      <title>Both sides report GREEN against their own copy of the shape. The wire between them is BROKEN.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="sp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="sp-arrow-bad" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path class="sp-head-bad" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <text class="sp-lane" x="4" y="12">PROVIDER</text>
      <rect class="sp-box" x="4" y="18" width="110" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="sp-title" x="59" y="40" text-anchor="middle">own fixture</text>
      <rect class="sp-ok" x="4" y="60" width="110" height="22" rx="2" />
      <text class="sp-ok-text" x="59" y="75" text-anchor="middle">GREEN</text>

      <text class="sp-lane" x="146" y="12">CONSUMER</text>
      <rect class="sp-box" x="146" y="18" width="110" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="sp-title" x="201" y="40" text-anchor="middle">own stub</text>
      <rect class="sp-ok" x="146" y="60" width="110" height="22" rx="2" />
      <text class="sp-ok-text" x="201" y="75" text-anchor="middle">GREEN</text>

      <path class="sp-edge" d="M 59 82 L 59 96" marker-end="url(#sp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="sp-edge" d="M 201 82 L 201 96" marker-end="url(#sp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="sp-edge-break" d="M 59 96 L 118 96" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="sp-edge-break" d="M 142 96 L 201 96" marker-end="url(#sp-arrow-bad)" aria-hidden="true" />

      <text class="sp-lane" x="4" y="116">THE WIRE</text>
      <rect class="sp-bad" x="4" y="122" width="252" height="22" rx="2" />
      <text class="sp-bad-text" x="130" y="137" text-anchor="middle">BROKEN</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="seam-plate-caption">
      Two unit tests that happen to agree are not a contract test. Each side
      was green. The seam was not.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <p class="toc-heading" id="toc-heading">On this page</p>
      <ol>
      <li><a href="#what-it-grew-into">What it grew into</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-map">The map</a></li>
      <li><a href="#how-it-flows">How it flows</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-problem">The problem</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-decisions">The decisions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-outcome">The outcome</a></li>
      <li><a href="#where-it-goes-next">Where it goes next</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <p>
      This is more process than a one-person project strictly needs. I know
      &mdash; I built it that way on purpose, as practice for the seam-level
      judgment that matters once repos belong to different teams. The system is
      small. I held the discipline anyway, and I wrote down the reasoning for
      every decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same discipline covers <em>how</em> the system gets built. The
      building is done by directing Claude Code, and that workflow has its own
      operating layer, public like the rest:
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops">agent-ops</a> &mdash;
      the <code>PreToolUse</code> guards that enforce the security posture
      mechanically, the working agreements parallel sessions run under, and an
      incident log held to a deliberate severity bar, failures left in.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-it-grew-into">What it grew into</h2>
    <ul class="repo-grid">
      <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/notes-api">notes-api</a></strong><span>FastAPI REST service. The knowledge base and data layer.</span></li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier">defense-news-classifier</a></strong><span>LLM classifier with a real eval harness. Labels the content.</span></li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/kb-agent">kb-agent</a></strong><span>RAG and tool-use agent. The hub that reasons over the system.</span></li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/learning-notes">learning-notes</a></strong><span>Plain-language notes on the techniques behind it all.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="ex-caption">All four repos are public &mdash; the ADRs and contract tests referenced below are checkable, not narrated.</p>

    <h2 id="the-map">The map</h2>
    <p>Click a box to see what each part does. The same flow is written out in words further down, so this is an enhancement, not a requirement.</p>
    <figure class="diagram-wrap">
      <svg class="diagram" id="system-diagram" viewBox="0 0 800 430" role="group" aria-label="Interactive map of the system: notes-api, classifier, and kb-agent, connected by an async BackgroundTasks enrichment loop.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g id="diagram-edges"></g>
        <g id="diagram-nodes"></g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="diagram-detail" id="diagram-detail" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">Click a box above to see what each part does.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 id="how-it-flows">How it flows</h2>
    <ol class="flow">
      <li>A note is created in <strong>notes-api</strong> (<code>POST /notes</code>), which enqueues a FastAPI <code>BackgroundTask</code> to classify and tag it. The <code>201</code> returns immediately; the task runs after the response is sent.</li>
      <li>The <strong>background task</strong> POSTs the note text to the classifier's <code>/classify</code> endpoint.</li>
      <li>The <strong>classifier</strong> classifies the text (one Sonnet call, structured output forced via tool-use) into a category, an operational domain, and a region, and returns them.</li>
      <li>The task writes the labels back as namespaced tags (<code>PUT /notes/&#123;id&#125;/tags</code>, replace semantics), preserving the user's own tags. It currently maps the first two; the region field is newer than this consumer. <code>CLASSIFIER_URL</code> unset makes the whole step a no-op.</li>
      <li>The <strong>kb-agent</strong> reads notes (<code>GET /notes</code>) to ground its RAG answers, and can also call the classifier synchronously.</li>
    </ol>


    <p class="diagram-more">
      That is the loop in words. <a class="card-link" href="the-system-run.html">Watch one note go through it, with the real schemas <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Once it was four repos, the easy path was to fuse them so they could call
      each other directly. That buys convenience and pays for it with independence:
      one release cycle, one blast radius, one thing to break. The goal was the
      opposite, to let them act as one system while each stays deployable on its
      own. The contracts between the pieces are where this problem actually
      lives.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-decisions">The decisions</h2>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-contracts">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; SYS-004 / SYS-006</p>
      <h3>Decouple with contracts, not shared code</h3>
      <p>The repos talk over frozen HTTP contracts with explicit
        versioning rules, and each side carries tests pinning the shape.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> decoupling without a contract is
        just hope. A renamed field breaks the consumer silently at runtime.
        <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> more upfront ceremony (freeze the wire shape,
        write the tests) so that drift fails a build instead of production.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Then it drifted anyway, and I want that on the
        page.</strong> I audited my own decision log in July 2026 and found this
        seam broken. The classifier shipped a third field; the frozen contract
        required a coordinated consumer update; that update never happened and
        both repos' CI stayed green. The reason is the part worth keeping: each
        side's &ldquo;contract test&rdquo; asserted against its <em>own</em> copy
        of the shape. The provider's fixture was updated in the same commit that
        changed the provider. The consumer checked itself against a stub it
        wrote. Neither could see the other, so the two suites agreed right up
        until they were both describing different systems.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What I actually learned:</strong> two unit
        tests that happen to agree are not a contract test, and calling them one
        bought me months of false confidence. A real one needs a single shared
        artifact both sides assert against, so changing one copy fails the
        other's build. The ceremony I'd paid for was the documentation half; the
        enforcement half was never wired. Amended in the open at
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/decisions/SYS-004-classify-http-contract.md">SYS-004</a>,
        with the falsified consequences struck through rather than deleted. It
        is not the only check I have caught reporting confidence it had not
        earned; <a href="false-green.html">False Green</a> collects six more, and
        <a href="context-architecture.html">Context Architecture</a> is the same
        split one layer up, where the rules an agent reads get promoted from
        prose into hooks.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-rightsized" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Decision &middot; SYS-005</p>
      <h3>Make the enrichment loop async and idempotent by design</h3>
      <p>Creating a note triggers a FastAPI BackgroundTask in notes-api. The task
        calls the classifier, then writes the returned labels back as namespaced
        tags (<code>category:</code> / <code>domain:</code>) with replace
        semantics — notes-api owns the writeback, the classifier stays a pure
        provider.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> running classification in a
        BackgroundTask keeps note creation fast and decouples it from classifier
        latency, while replace semantics ensure reprocessing converges instead of
        accumulating duplicate tags. <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> making the loop
        idempotent took real design work. Each task needs its own retry path and
        its own error handling, so it's more than firing something off and
        forgetting it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-eval">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; SYS-002</p>
      <h3>Gate model choice on evidence, not vibes</h3>
      <p>Default to Sonnet across the system. Escalate to Opus only where an eval
        shows the quality gain pays for itself. The same gate ran the July 2026
        migration to Sonnet 5: eval before and after on the same gold set.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> the escalation has now been built
        and measured three separate times, and declined all three &mdash; the
        verdicts are on the
        <a href="defense-news-classifier.html">classifier</a> and the
        <a href="faithfulness-judge.html">judge</a> pages, where they were
        measured. <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> you have to run the eval to
        justify the tier instead of defaulting to the biggest model.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; SYS-001 / ADR-001</p>
      <h3>Record decisions, then make the system legible</h3>
      <p>Cross-repo decisions live as two-tier ADRs (repo-local choices stay local;
        system-level ones are <code>SYS-NNN</code> in one place). A generated
        portal pulls every repo's docs into one browsable view that deep-links
        back to the code. It links to each doc where it already lives instead of
        copying it, so there's a single source of truth.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> a system you cannot see in one place
        is narrated, not legible, and a decision that is not written down gets
        re-argued.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="the-outcome">The outcome</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Create a note and it is automatically classified and tagged</strong>, asynchronously, with no coupling between note creation and classifier availability.</li>
      <li><strong>The four repos stay independently deployable</strong> &mdash; though cross-repo drift is <em>not</em> yet caught by a red build. I thought it was; an audit proved otherwise, and closing that gap needs one shared contract artifact both sides assert against.</li>
      <li><strong>The whole system is browsable in one portal</strong>, with links straight to the real code.</li>
      <li><strong>A skill map (SYS-007) spells out what the system exercises</strong>: evals, context engineering, agents, and observability. It also names where I'm weak, which is security on the agent's tool seam.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No one designs a perfect system upfront, including me. What I did do, as it
      grew, was keep applying the same discipline at every seam: freeze the
      contract, make it idempotent, write down why, check the evidence instead of
      guessing. The ADRs and contract tests back this up.
    </p>

    <h2 id="where-it-goes-next">Where it goes next</h2>
    <p>
      This page is the horizontal view: four repos and the contracts on the
      seams between them. The roadmap is vertical &mdash; an autonomy ladder that
      hands the classifier more self-direction one level at a time, and two of
      its four levels shipped a &ldquo;no.&rdquo;
      <a class="card-link" href="autonomy-ladder.html">The ladder, level by level <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </p>
  </main>
